In Terry Pratchett's Excellent, _The Last Hero_, he
has the character Hughnon Ridcully, Chief Priest of
Blind Io, musing:

    Gods were very much a fashion thing.  Look
    at Om, now. One minute he was a bloodthirsty
    little deity in some mad hot country, and
    then suddenly he was one of the top gods.
    It had all been done by NOT answering
    prayers, but doing so in a sort of _dynamic_
    way that left open the possibility that one
    day he might and _then_ there'd be
    fireworks.  Hughnon, who had survived
    through decades of intense theological
    dispute by being a mean man at swinging a
    heavy thurible, was impressed by this novel
    technique.

NetHack has always been under pressure from software
impulses in two directions. One, horrified that any
player would even dream of ascending, has produced
Slash'em, the version designed to be always actively
looking for ways to kill you, as one poster here
more or less described it.

The other, from players damn-all tired of reading
only of _other_ people's ascensions (and not
understanding that these "people" are actually
mutant space beings blowing their cover as spies on
Earth), pushes always to make NetHack into _NetHack,
Land of the Fluffy Bunnies_ by finding ways to make
the game less dangerous.  Vanilla NetHack, again
paraphrasing what I'm too lazy to go find and quote
properly, doesn't really care if you live or die,
just provides you abundant opportunities to choose
the latter option.

I follow Hughnon's thinking on this: NetHack keeps
my attention by never letting me ascend, but leaving
open always the possibility that I **might** in the
future, and +then+ there'd be partying.

Rings against Death Magic would be wonderfully
suitable for the Fluffy Bunny version of NetHack,
but it would remove all my own interest in the game
by destroying the current dynamic.

xanthian, playing since 1984 when NetHack was in
short pants and called "Rogue". Based on reality and
my perfect track record of finding increasingly
stupider ways to die, I'm never going to ascend in
Vanilla, but seemingly I keep getting so very, very
*close*!



